From 585c8e0411d0d1ca3b63c3b7a82553f50a191917 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 21:15:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08104/11362] Content failures eat the attempts the defect
 gates run on, so the gates go quiet on hard games

The incident, found in a live A/B's first treatment cell rather than in review: ar25 accepted a
textbook `return False` -- the model's own comment reads "no win state was given ... maybe just
return False" -- with `goal_defect_reasks == 0` AND `engine_defect_reasks == 0`. Both gates silent
at once is what pointed at a shared cause rather than a bug in either.

`attempt < tries - 1` guards both, for a real reason: a `continue` on the final attempt falls out
of the loop into the content-failure return, converting an accept into a hard failure. But `tries`
is ALSO the budget CONTENT failures draw from -- no code block, missing `def`, syntax error. So
every attempt spent on malformed output is an attempt the defect gate needed, and an answer that
finally parses on the last attempt is never checked at all. The gate is quietest exactly where it
is most needed: a hard game burns its attempts on unusable output, then lands its one parseable
answer where nothing is armed.

NOT A REGRESSION FROM THE NEWER GOAL GATE. The shipped ENGINE gate carries the identical guard and
the identical blind spot, so its measured 13/36 -> 22/36 is a FLOOR on that gate, not a ceiling.

THE FIX GRANTS AN ATTEMPT RATHER THAN RELAXING THE GUARD. Under
CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_DEFECT_OWNS_ATTEMPTS a defect re-ask extends the budget instead of consuming a
content attempt. That keeps the "never fails where the old path succeeded" guarantee true by
construction -- the change only ever ADDS an iteration and cannot turn an accept into a failure --
and it needs no cap, because the grant is gated on `_reasks_left` / `_goal_reasks_left`, which are
finite. A test pins that bound rather than trusting it: 3 content attempts + at most 2 grants = 5
server calls, asserted.

DEFAULT OFF, and that is the load-bearing part. The A/B that FOUND this was still collecting when
the fix was written; flipping it mid-run would have measured two treatments under one label. The
test therefore asserts BOTH directions -- with the flag off the bug REPRODUCES exactly (a
content-failure sequence still yields zero defect re-asks), and only with it on does the gate
fire. A clean answer costs exactly one call in both modes.

A FIXTURE I GOT WRONG, recorded because the test caught it and I did not. The first "defective"
engine returned `grid.copy()` on every path -- the IDENTITY function, which this module's own
docstring documents as clearing every check while modelling nothing. It is not a defect, so it
could not exercise a defect gate, and two tests failed for that reason rather than for a code
fault. Replaced with the shape the 2026-07-30 audit actually measured on ft09: the `action == 6`
path computes and falls off the end with no return.

72 tests pass across the induce, re-ask, static-validation and win-transition suites. 7 artifacts
acknowledged; the evidence names the DEFAULT-OFF proof rather than reusing the win-transition
byte-identity argument, which was written first and describes a different mechanism -- reverted
and rewritten rather than left approximately right.

Banked levels remain 3/3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw
